# Splunk Detection Queries

## 1. Suricata Alert Events

```spl
index=* sourcetype="suricata:eve" event_type="alert"
| table _time src_ip dest_ip alert.signature alert.severity alert.category
| sort - _time
```

Purpose: Review Suricata-generated security alerts.

## 2. Group Events by Source IP

```spl
index=* sourcetype="suricata:eve" event_type="alert"
| stats count by src_ip
| sort - count
```

Purpose: Identify source IPs generating the highest number of alerts.

## 3. Review Alert Signatures

```spl
index=* sourcetype="suricata:eve" event_type="alert"
| stats count by alert.signature alert.severity
| sort - count
```

Purpose: Summarize alert signatures and severity.

## 4. SSH-Related Suricata Events

```spl
index=* sourcetype="suricata:eve"
("ssh" OR "22")
| table _time src_ip dest_ip dest_port event_type alert.signature
| sort - _time
```

Purpose: Investigate events potentially related to SSH activity. Confirm the event fields and actual detection rules before treating a result as a brute-force detection.

## 5. Alert Configuration

The Splunk alert was configured to send matching search results to the Flask webhook endpoint.

Example local lab endpoint:

`http://127.0.0.1:5000/webhook`

Note: This loopback address is suitable only when Splunk and Flask can reach each other on the same host. Use the reachable Flask host address when they run on different machines.
